CHAPTER 12
Venue and Transfer
In this chapter on venue and transfer, we continue our discussion of how procedure defines the “where” of litigation. Venue refers to the place where a lawsuit may be heard. Within a given court system, and even among those courts with jurisdiction over the case and the parties, a venue statute may require that a case be filed in a certain judicial district. Transfer, in this context, refers to the power of a court to send a case to another court within the same court system. Our focus is on the federal court system, and therefore in this chapter we will study the federal venue statute and the federal transfer statute. We also study the ancient common law doctrine of forum non conveniens—roughly (and unsurprisingly) translated, “the forum is not convenient”—which the federal courts still use today. Under this doctrine, a federal court may dismiss an action because there is another forum that is better suited to hear the case.

Venue, transfer, and forum non conveniens all address the question of what is the proper place for a case to be heard, even among those courts that have jurisdiction. These topics are akin to personal jurisdiction, in that they account for inconvenience to the parties and the connection between the dispute and the forum court.

They also lead us toward out next topic, the Erie problem. When a case moves from one court to another, whether by transfer or by dismissal and refiling due to forum non conveniens, a choice-of-law problem arises: Does the court in which the case was first filed decide which law governs the plaintiff’s claims, or does the court that ends up with the case decide? Sometimes, of course, both courts would look at the case and choose the same state’s (or country’s) law. But what if they were to disagree? This choice-of-law problem foreshadows the increasingly complex choice-of-law issues we will encounter in the next chapter.

